First, I need to determine the measures of the two complementary angles. Since their ratio is 4 to 5, I'll set up the problem using algebra. Let 4x represent one angle and 5x represent the other. Because they are complementary, their sum is 90 degrees.

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
4x + 5x &= 90^\circ \\
9x &= 90^\circ \\
x &= 10^\circ
\end{aligned}
}

Now, the smaller angle is 4x, which is 40 degrees, and the larger angle is 5x, which is 50 degrees.

Next, the smaller angle is increased by 10%. To find the increase:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{Increase} &= 40^\circ \times 10\% = 4^\circ \\
\text{New smaller angle} &= 40^\circ + 4^\circ = 44^\circ
\end{aligned}
}

The sum of the two angles must still be 90 degrees for them to be complementary. Therefore, the new larger angle must be:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{New larger angle} &= 90^\circ - 44^\circ = 46^\circ
\end{aligned}
}

Finally, to find the percentage decrease needed for the larger angle:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{Percentage decrease} &= \frac{50^\circ - 46^\circ}{50^\circ} \times 100\% = \frac{4^\circ}{50^\circ} \times 100\% = 8\%
\end{aligned}
}
