To solve this, consider that the repeating decimal 0.abab… can be expressed as the fraction (10a + b)/99. Since a and b are digits, 10a + b ranges from 1 to 99. The number of distinct denominators corresponds to the number of distinct reduced forms of (10a + b)/99. The denominator will be 99 divided by the greatest common divisor (GCD) of (10a + b) and 99. The number of distinct denominators is equal to the number of divisors of 99 that are GCDs of (10a + b) and 99. The divisors of 99 are 1, 3, 9, 11, 33, and 99. Therefore, there are 6 different possible denominators.

