To maximize the expression \( A \cdot M \cdot C + A \cdot M + M \cdot C + C \cdot A \) given that \( A + M + C = 12 \), we can proceed as follows.

First, note that the expression can be rewritten as \( (A + 1)(M + 1)(C + 1) - (A + M + C + 1) \). Since \( A + M + C = 12 \), this simplifies to \( (A + 1)(M + 1)(C + 1) - 13 \). To maximize the original expression, we need to maximize \( (A + 1)(M + 1)(C + 1) \) under the constraint \( A + M + C = 12 \).

By the AM-GM inequality, the product \( (A + 1)(M + 1)(C + 1) \) is maximized when \( A = M = C = 4 \). Plugging these values in, we get \( (5)(5)(5) = 125 \). Therefore, the maximum value of the original expression is \( 125 - 13 = 112 \).

