To determine the two-digit positive integer \( t \) such that the last two digits of \( 11 \cdot t \) are 36, we can set up the congruence equation:

\[
11t \equiv 36 \pmod{100}
\]

We can solve for \( t \) by finding the modular inverse of 11 modulo 100. The modular inverse of 11 is 91 because \( 11 \times 91 = 1001 \equiv 1 \pmod{100} \). Multiplying both sides of the equation by 91:

\[
t \equiv 36 \times 91 \pmod{100}
\]
\[
t \equiv 3276 \pmod{100}
\]
\[
t \equiv 76 \pmod{100}
\]

Since \( t \) is a two-digit number, \( t = 76 \).

