To find the minimum positive value of the sum \(\sum_{1 \le i < j \le 100} a_i a_j\) where each \(a_k\) is either 1 or -1, we first note that this sum can be expressed as \(\frac{(S)^2 - 100}{2}\) where \(S = \sum_{k=1}^{100} a_k\). Since each \(a_k\) is ±1, \(S\) must be an even integer between -100 and 100. The expression \(\frac{S^2 - 100}{2}\) is non-negative when \(S^2 \ge 100\), i.e., when \(|S| \ge 10\). The smallest even integer satisfying \(|S| \ge 10\) is 10 itself. Plugging \(S = 10\) into the expression gives \(\frac{100 - 100}{2} = 0\), which is not positive. The next possible value is \(S = 12\), resulting in \(\frac{144 - 100}{2} = 22\). Thus, the minimum positive value is 22.

